 "news": {
  "date": "2026-10-05",
  "asof": "2026-10-06 11:46 UTC",
  "headline": "The Nasdaq closed at a record and the long end made a 52-week high \u2014 off the same report",
  "standfirst": "ISM services came in at 54.9 against 55.0 expected, but its prices index rose 1.4 points to 74.0 against 72.9 expected \u2014 a second month above 72 \u2014 while employment fell to 50.1. The two ends of the curve each took a side: the two-year fell to 4.823% while the ten-year rose 3.8 basis points to 5.315% and the thirty-year 4.0 to 5.670%, both fresh 52-week highs, with the ten-year touching 5.35% intraday. That is the fourth steepening in five sessions. Equities went the other way entirely: the Nasdaq closed at a record 27,477.31, up 1.05%, and the S&P is 0.3% from its all-time high, lifted by two AI-pitched buyouts \u2014 Schneider Electric for PTC at $22.6bn and C.H. Robinson for RXO. In the book GOOG rose 1.02% to 343.83, its best close since entry, and OKTA rose 3.22% to 218.29 on another 52-week high with no news at all, reaching +1.77R. Heat 3.05R against 3.0R, unchanged for an eighteenth session.",
  "themes": [
   {
    "kicker": "What's next",
    "right": "CPI on the 13th \u00b7 then 28 October",
    "title": "One print before the Fed, and nothing from the companies until the 28th",
    "items": [
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "CPI",
      "html": "<b>September CPI</b> \u2014 the only first-tier print before the Fed, and now the test of two rising ISM prices indices",
      "when": "Tue \u00b7 2026-10-13",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FOMC",
      "html": "<b>Fed decision</b> \u2014 October hike odds collapsed to 16%; the long end has made new highs since",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "GOOG",
      "html": "<b>Alphabet Q3 earnings</b> \u2014 the first read on $195-205bn of capex and on Suncatcher, the same day as the Fed",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>Apple Q4 earnings</b> \u2014 the first iPhone 18 quarter and the first read on Ternus",
      "when": "Thu \u00b7 2026-10-29",
      "kind": "calendar"
     }
    ],
    "note": "<b>September CPI on the 13th is the one thing that can settle this week's argument.</b> Two ISM prices indices have accelerated inside three sessions while core PCE missed to the downside. CPI is the referee. A hot print validates the long end and the <b>5.670% thirty-year</b> stops looking like a supply story and starts looking like an inflation one; a soft print leaves the long end rising on nothing identifiable, which is the harder version of the problem because there is no release to trade against it."
   }
  ],
